1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,100
To ensure that Burp Suite Enterprise Edition can function correctly, you may need to configure

2
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:12,020
your firewall to allow the various components to communicate with each other and the public

3
00:00:12,020 --> 00:00:13,680
web.

4
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:17,720
If you want to deploy Burp Suite Enterprise Edition to a single machine, you need to ensure

5
00:00:17,720 --> 00:00:20,280
that the following connections are allowed.

6
00:00:20,280 --> 00:00:23,580
Allow your users and API clients to access the web server.

7
00:00:23,580 --> 00:00:26,680
By default, they use port 8080.

8
00:00:26,680 --> 00:00:31,160
On standard deployments, you can choose a different port during the installation process.

9
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:35,240
You can't change the web server port on a Kubernetes deployment, as your external port

10
00:00:35,240 --> 00:00:38,800
should be configured as part of your Ingress solution.

11
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:43,640
To activate your license and enable automatic software updates, allow the Enterprise server

12
00:00:43,640 --> 00:00:47,920
to access port swigger.net on port 443.

13
00:00:47,920 --> 00:00:53,620
If necessary, configure a network proxy to reach the public web.

14
00:00:53,620 --> 00:01:00,160
These settings are available by accessing the cog in Burp Suite Enterprise UI.

15
00:01:00,160 --> 00:01:06,540
To allow email notifications, give the Enterprise server access to your SMTP server.

16
00:01:06,540 --> 00:01:15,480
To find the correct port number for your email service, refer to your email service provider.

17
00:01:15,480 --> 00:01:21,480
Allow the machine to access websites that you want to scan on ports 80 and 443, via a proxy

18
00:01:21,480 --> 00:01:24,260
server if necessary.

19
00:01:24,260 --> 00:01:29,020
To gain the full benefit of Burp Scanner's out-of-band vulnerability detection technology,

20
00:01:29,020 --> 00:01:41,600
allow the machine to access burpcollaborates.net and oastify.com on ports 80 and 443.

21
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:45,700
This screenshot shows a multi-system deployment.

22
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:49,360
For this type of deployment, configure the connections as follows.

23
00:01:49,360 --> 00:01:52,420
Allow your users and API clients to access the web server.

24
00:01:52,420 --> 00:01:55,280
By default, they use port 8080.

25
00:01:55,280 --> 00:01:59,660
To activate your license and perform automatic software updates, allow the Enterprise server

26
00:01:59,660 --> 00:02:03,140
to access portswigger.net on port 443.

27
00:02:03,140 --> 00:02:08,520
If necessary, configure a network proxy to reach the public web.

28
00:02:08,520 --> 00:02:13,960
Allow your scanning machines to access the Enterprise server machine on port 8072.

29
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:18,640
Allow the Enterprise server to access portswigger.net throughout the scanning machine installation

30
00:02:18,640 --> 00:02:19,640
process.

31
00:02:19,640 --> 00:02:24,140
This is necessary to activate the scanning machine license.

32
00:02:24,140 --> 00:02:29,900
Allow your scanning machines to access the websites that you want to scan on ports 80 and 443.

33
00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:34,200
And if you use a bundle database, allow any external scanning machines to access the Enterprise

34
00:02:34,200 --> 00:02:38,360
server machine on port 9092.

35
00:02:38,360 --> 00:02:43,400
If you use an external database, allow the Enterprise server and any external scanning machines to

36
00:02:43,400 --> 00:02:48,920
have access to the database server on the configured host and port.

37
00:02:48,920 --> 00:02:54,200
We recommend that you create a dedicated DMZ network to host the machines that Burp Suite Enterprise

38
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:55,720
Edition is deployed on.

39
00:02:55,720 --> 00:02:57,220
However, this isn't mandatory.

